<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thor</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Donar, Thunor</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thor was the Germanic god of thunder, known to the continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> as Thunaer or Donar and to the English as Thunor. Armed with his hammer Mjolnir, he was the protector of gods and men against giants and the most widely worshipped god of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>. The Old Saxon baptismal vow required converts to renounce him, and his name survives in Thursday.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thor.jpg" alt="Thor" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Max Koch · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_painting_by_Max_Friedrich_Koch_-_Donar-Thor.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Religion was pagan: a Saxon baptismal vow of the late 8th or 9th century makes converts renounce <b>Thunaer</b>, Woden and Saxnot, and sacred sites included springs, groves and the Irminsul, a great pillar or tree near the Eresburg, destroyed by Charlemagne in 772.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saxons">Saxons · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>A soapstone mould found in Denmark, from the 10th century, was made to cast both Christian crosses and the hammers of <b>Thor</b>, so that a smith could sell to customers of either faith.</blockquote><a href="/religion/norse-religion">Norse religion · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Sámi religion shares some elements with Norse mythology, possibly from early contact with Viking traders, and contemporary ethnographers counted Sámi among the last worshippers of <b>Thor</b> in the 18th century.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sami">Sámi · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>…and kings, who held sacrificial feasts, blót, at their halls and at sacred places, and the gods were many, among them Odin, god of wisdom, poetry and the dead in battle, <b>Thor</b>, the thunderer who guarded gods and people, and Freyr and Freyja, who gave fertility and plenty.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>After 101 days and some 7,000 km from Peru, <b>Thor</b> Heyerdahl's balsa raft ran onto the reef at Raroia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-7">7 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
